const EXPLAIN={
1:{"how": ["<b>x-intercepts</b> are where the graph crosses the x-axis. Their <var>y</var> is always 0: <b>(−10, 0)</b> and <b>(2, 0)</b>.", "The <b>y-intercept</b> is where it crosses the y-axis. Its <var>x</var> is always 0: <b>(0, −5)</b>.", "That is D."], "why": {"0": "This puts the y-intercept number where the x-intercepts go and the x-intercept numbers where the y-intercept goes, with the signs switched. Read the graph: it crosses the x-axis at −10 and 2, and the y-axis at −5.", "1": "The numbers are right, but the ordered pairs are backwards. An x-intercept has y = 0, like (−10, 0). The y-intercept is (0, −5).", "2": "The x- and y-intercepts are switched and the signs are flipped. The graph crosses the x-axis at −10 and 2."}, "right": "x-intercept: (number, 0). y-intercept: (0, number)."},
2:{"how": ["Pick two rows: (5, 20) and (7, 28).", "<b>Run</b> (minutes): 7 − 5 = 2. <b>Rise</b> (items): 28 − 20 = 8.", "Rate = 8 ÷ 2 = <b>4 items per minute</b>. That is C.", "Check: every row is items = 4 × minutes (5 × 4 = 20, 14 × 4 = 56). ✓"], "why": {"0": "8 is the change in items, but that happened over 2 minutes. Divide: 8 ÷ 2 = 4.", "1": "37 is the average of the items column. Rate of change is change in items ÷ change in minutes.", "3": "This subtracts 20 − 5 in the first row. Rate of change compares two rows."}, "right": "Change in y ÷ change in x.", "numworks": "<b>Regression</b>: put x in X1 and y in Y1, open the Graph tab, press OK and pick <b>Linear ax+b</b>. The Stats tab shows a = 4, b = 0."},
3:{"how": ["Line going up: it crosses the y-axis at <b>−3</b>. From (−5, −5) to (0, −3): run 5, rise 2, slope <b><span class=\"frac\"><span>2</span><span>5</span></span></b>. So <var>y</var> = <span class=\"frac\"><span>2</span><span>5</span></span><var>x</var> − 3.", "Line going down: it crosses the y-axis at <b>−8</b>. From (−5, −5) to (0, −8): run 5, rise −3, slope <b>−<span class=\"frac\"><span>3</span><span>5</span></span></b>. So <var>y</var> = −<span class=\"frac\"><span>3</span><span>5</span></span><var>x</var> − 8.", "That is B."], "why": {"0": "The slopes are right, but the y-intercepts are switched. The line going up crosses the y-axis at −3.", "2": "The slopes are upside down (run over rise) and the y-intercepts are switched. Slope is rise over run.", "3": "The y-intercepts are right, but the slopes are upside down. Slope is rise over run: 2/5 and −3/5."}, "right": "b is where each line crosses the y-axis; slope is rise over run.", "desmos": "Type <b>y = (2/5)x − 3</b> and <b>y = −(3/5)x − 8</b>. They match the graph and cross at (−5, −5)."},
4:{"how": ["A graph is a function if every <var>x</var> has only <b>one</b> <var>y</var>.", "<b>Vertical line test</b>: slide a vertical line across the graph. If it ever hits the graph twice, it is <b>not</b> a function.", "A, B and C each have places where a vertical line hits two or three points. D passes: every vertical line hits it only once. That is D."], "why": {"0": "This parabola opens sideways. The line x = 0 hits it at y = 6 and y = −6, so it fails the vertical line test.", "1": "This parabola opens sideways. The line x = 0 hits it twice, so it fails the vertical line test.", "2": "At x = 0 this graph has three y-values (6, 0 and −6). It fails the vertical line test."}, "right": "Vertical line test: one hit everywhere = function."},
5:{"how": ["Multiply the first equation by 2: 4<var>x</var> + 2<var>y</var> = 80.", "Add it to the second: 4<var>x</var> + <var>x</var> = 5<var>x</var>, and 2<var>y</var> − 2<var>y</var> = 0, and 80 + (−20) = 60. So 5<var>x</var> = 60 and <var>x</var> = <b>12</b>.", "Put <var>x</var> = 12 in the first: 24 + <var>y</var> = 40, so <var>y</var> = <b>16</b>.", "Solution (12, 16). That is A.", "Check: 12 − 2(16) = 12 − 32 = −20. ✓"], "why": {"1": "Check it: 2(15) + 17.5 = 47.5, not 40.", "2": "The lines have different slopes (−2 and 1/2), so they cross. There is exactly one solution.", "3": "Infinite solutions only happen when both equations are the same line. These lines have different slopes."}, "right": "Elimination: make one variable cancel, then plug back in. Always check both equations.", "desmos": "Type <b>y = −2x + 40</b> and <b>y = (1/2)x + 10</b>. Click where they cross: (12, 16).", "numworks": "<b>Grapher</b>: Add an element, type −2x + 40 and x/2 + 10, open the Graph tab, press the <b>Toolbox</b> key and choose <b>Intersection</b>: (12, 16)."},
6:{"how": ["<b>Range</b> is the <var>y</var>-values. “Greater than or equal to 3” means the lowest <var>y</var> is <b>3</b> and the graph goes <b>up</b> from there.", "So the parabola must open <b>up</b> with its vertex (lowest point) at <var>y</var> = 3.", "Only D does that: vertex (−2, 3), opening up. That is D."], "why": {"0": "This opens down and its highest point is at y = −3. Its range is y ≤ −3.", "1": "The vertex is at y = 3, but it opens down. Its range is y ≤ 3 (3 and below).", "2": "This opens up, but its lowest point is at y = −3, not 3. Its range is y ≥ −3."}, "right": "Range = y-values. Find the lowest or highest point, then which way it opens."},
7:{"how": ["Directly proportional: <var>y</var> = <var>k</var><var>x</var>. Find <var>k</var> with the pair you know: <var>k</var> = <span class=\"frac\"><span><var>y</var></span><span><var>x</var></span></span> = <span class=\"frac\"><span>128</span><span>512</span></span> = <b><span class=\"frac\"><span>1</span><span>4</span></span></b>.", "Now <var>x</var> = 64: <var>y</var> = <span class=\"frac\"><span>1</span><span>4</span></span>(64) = <b>16</b>. That is C."], "why": {"0": "This divides 512 by 128 to get k = 4. k is y ÷ x: 128 ÷ 512 = 1/4.", "1": "k = 1/4 is right, but it was multiplied by 128. Use the new x: (1/4)(64) = 16.", "3": "This divides 512 by 64. Find k first (y ÷ x = 1/4), then multiply by 64."}, "right": "k = y ÷ x, then y = kx.", "numworks": "<b>Calculation</b>: type 128/512×64. It shows 16."},
8:{"how": ["The boundary line 8<var>x</var> + 5<var>y</var> = 100 goes through (0, 20) and (12.5, 0).", "≤ has “or equal,” so the line is <b>solid</b>.", "Test (0, 0): 8(0) + 5(0) = 0, and 0 ≤ 100 is true. So shade the side <b>with</b> (0, 0), below the line. That is B.", "It makes sense: buying fewer towels costs less, so the points under the line are the ones that fit the $100."], "why": {"0": "This is dashed (≤ needs a solid line) and shaded above. The points above the line cost more than $100.", "2": "The line is solid, but it is shaded above. Test (0, 0): 0 ≤ 100 is true, so shade the side with (0, 0).", "3": "The shading is right (below), but the line should be solid. ≤ means spending exactly $100 is allowed."}, "right": "Solid or dashed from the symbol, then test (0, 0) to pick the side.", "desmos": "Desmos on the test can’t graph inequalities. Type <b>y = −(8/5)x + 20</b> to see the line, then test (0, 0) yourself."},
9:{"how": ["The graph crosses the y-axis at <b>1</b>, so <var>a</var> = 1.", "Going right, the y-values get smaller fast, so <var>b</var> is between 0 and 1 (decay).", "Read points: at <var>x</var> = −1, <var>y</var> = 4. At <var>x</var> = −2, <var>y</var> = 16. Each step to the left multiplies by 4, so each step right multiplies by <span class=\"frac\"><span>1</span><span>4</span></span> = <b>0.25</b>.", "<var>p</var>(<var>x</var>) = (0.25)<sup><var>x</var></sup>. That is A."], "why": {"1": "This starts at 2 when x = 0. The graph crosses the y-axis at 1.", "2": "1.25 is greater than 1, so this grows. The graph decreases from left to right.", "3": "25 is greater than 1, so this grows very fast. The graph goes down from left to right."}, "right": "a = y-intercept. b less than 1 means it goes down; b more than 1 means it goes up.", "desmos": "Type <b>y = 0.25^x</b>. It goes through (0, 1), (−1, 4) and (−2, 16), like the graph."},
10:{"how": ["Multiply every term by every term: <var>n</var> · 2<var>n</var> = 2<var>n</var><sup>2</sup>, <var>n</var> · 7 = 7<var>n</var>, −4 · 2<var>n</var> = −8<var>n</var>, −4 · 7 = −28.", "Combine the middle terms: 7<var>n</var> − 8<var>n</var> = <b>−<var>n</var></b>.", "2<var>n</var><sup>2</sup> − <var>n</var> − 28. That is D."], "why": {"0": "This adds the two expressions. The parentheses next to each other mean multiply.", "1": "n · 2n is 2n², not 2n. Keep the squared term.", "2": "7n − 8n is −n, not −15n."}, "right": "Four multiplications, then combine like terms."},
11:{"how": ["<b>Causation</b> means one thing actually makes the other happen.", "An hourly worker is paid for each hour. Fewer hours <b>cause</b> less money. That is B."], "why": {"0": "People on a bus have nothing to do with zoo animals. There is no connection.", "2": "A bigger discount might change sales, but it doesn’t have to make sales go down. That is not a sure cause.", "3": "Bike trails can’t change how much it rains. There is no connection."}, "right": "Ask: does the first thing directly make the second thing happen?"},
12:{"how": ["Line <var>h</var> (table): (−8, 1) to (−4, −2): run 4, rise −3, slope <b>−<span class=\"frac\"><span>3</span><span>4</span></span></b>. Using (−4, −2): −2 = −<span class=\"frac\"><span>3</span><span>4</span></span>(−4) + <var>b</var> = 3 + <var>b</var>, so <var>b</var> = <b>−5</b>.", "Line <var>j</var> (graph): (−4, 6) to (4, −4): run 8, rise −10, slope <b>−<span class=\"frac\"><span>5</span><span>4</span></span></b>. It crosses the y-axis at <b>1</b>.", "<var>y</var> = −<span class=\"frac\"><span>3</span><span>4</span></span><var>x</var> − 5 and <var>y</var> = −<span class=\"frac\"><span>5</span><span>4</span></span><var>x</var> + 1. That is D."], "why": {"0": "The slopes are upside down and positive. Both lines go down, so both slopes are negative: rise over run.", "1": "The slopes are positive, but both lines go down. Both slopes are negative.", "2": "The slopes are upside down (run over rise). Slope is rise over run: −3/4 and −5/4."}, "right": "Both lines go down → both slopes negative. Rise over run.", "desmos": "Type <b>y = −(3/4)x − 5</b>. It goes through every point in the table. Then <b>y = −(5/4)x + 1</b> goes through (−4, 6) and (4, −4)."},
13:{"how": ["Subtracting 1 <b>outside</b> the <var>x</var><sup>2</sup> takes every <var>y</var>-value down 1.", "So the graph moves <b>down 1</b>. That is C."], "why": {"0": "−1 outside makes every y-value 1 smaller, so the graph moves down, not up.", "1": "A sideways shift needs a change inside, like (x + 1)². The −1 is outside.", "3": "A sideways shift needs a change inside, like (x − 1)². The −1 is outside."}, "right": "Outside: up or down as it looks. Inside: sideways, opposite the sign."},
14:{"how": ["Find two numbers that multiply to <b>−6</b> and add to <b>−1</b> (the number in front of <var>d</var>): <b>2</b> and <b>−3</b>.", "<var>d</var><sup>2</sup> − <var>d</var> − 6 = (<var>d</var> + 2)(<var>d</var> − 3).", "Match it to (<var>d</var> + 2)(<var>d</var> + <var>k</var>): <var>k</var> = <b>−3</b>."], "miss": "The answer is −3, not 3. (d − 3) is the same as (d + (−3)), so k = −3. Check: 2 × (−3) = −6 and 2 + (−3) = −1.", "right": "Multiply to the last number, add to the middle number.", "check": "Multiply back: (d + 2)(d − 3) = d² − 3d + 2d − 6 = d² − d − 6. ✓"},
15:{"how": ["The line crosses the y-axis at <b>7</b>, so <var>b</var> = 7.", "Use the two points (4, 0) and (8, −7): run 4, rise −7. Slope = <b>−<span class=\"frac\"><span>7</span><span>4</span></span></b>.", "<var>y</var> = −<span class=\"frac\"><span>7</span><span>4</span></span><var>x</var> + 7. That is B."], "why": {"0": "The slope is right, but 4 is where the line crosses the x-axis. b is where it crosses the y-axis: 7.", "2": "The slope is upside down (run over rise), and 4 is the x-intercept, not the y-intercept.", "3": "b = 7 is right, but the slope is upside down. Rise over run: −7/4."}, "right": "b on the y-axis, slope = rise over run."},
16:{"how": ["Power to a power: (<var>d</var><sup>6</sup>)<sup>3</sup> = <var>d</var><sup>6 × 3</sup> = <b><var>d</var><sup>18</sup></b>.", "Dividing powers: <span class=\"frac\"><span><var>c</var><sup>8</sup></span><span><var>c</var><sup>2</sup></span></span> = <var>c</var><sup>8 − 2</sup> = <b><var>c</var><sup>6</sup></b>.", "<var>c</var><sup>6</sup><var>d</var><sup>18</sup>. That is D."], "why": {"0": "This divides 8 ÷ 2 for c and adds 6 + 3 for d. Subtract when dividing (8 − 2), multiply for a power of a power (6 × 3).", "1": "d¹⁸ is right, but dividing powers means subtract the exponents: 8 − 2 = 6.", "2": "c⁶ is right, but a power of a power multiplies: 6 × 3 = 18."}, "right": "Multiply powers: add. Divide: subtract. Power of a power: multiply."},
17:{"how": ["Get 0 on one side: 5<var>x</var><sup>2</sup> − 30<var>x</var> + 45 = 0.", "Pull out the 5: 5(<var>x</var><sup>2</sup> − 6<var>x</var> + 9) = 0.", "Factor: <var>x</var><sup>2</sup> − 6<var>x</var> + 9 = (<var>x</var> − 3)<sup>2</sup>, so <var>x</var> − 3 = 0 and <var>x</var> = <b>3</b>. That is A.", "Check: 5(9) = 45 and 30(3) − 45 = 45. ✓"], "why": {"1": "The factor is (x − 3)², so x = 3. A sign mix-up gives −3. Check: 5(9) = 45, but 30(−3) − 45 = −135.", "2": "5 is the number pulled out in front, not a solution. Keep factoring: (x − 3)² = 0.", "3": "5 is the number pulled out in front, not a solution. Check: 5(25) = 125, but 30(−5) − 45 = −195."}, "right": "0 on one side, factor, then set each factor to 0.", "desmos": "Type <b>y = 5x^2</b> and <b>y = 30x − 45</b>. They touch at one point: (3, 45). The solution is x = 3.", "numworks": "<b>Equations</b>: type the equation (with =), then <b>Solve the equation</b>: x = 3."},
18:{"how": ["<b>Domain</b> is the <var>x</var>-values (DLR: domain goes left to right).", "<var>x</var> is the number of bouquets. It can be 0, 1, 2, … up to 8, and only whole numbers (you can’t make half a bouquet).", "{0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8}. That is B."], "why": {"0": "These are the numbers on the x-axis scale, not the bouquets the florist can make. 10 is more than 8, and 1, 3, 5, 7 are missing.", "2": "These are the numbers on the y-axis scale. Domain is the x-values.", "3": "These are the y-values (flowers used). That is the range. Domain is the x-values (bouquets)."}, "right": "Domain = x (DLR). Range = y (RBT)."},
19:{"how": ["Use the two dots: (−3, 5) and (9, −5).", "<b>Run</b>: 9 − (−3) = 12. <b>Rise</b>: −5 − 5 = −10.", "Slope = <span class=\"frac\"><span>−10</span><span>12</span></span> = <b>−<span class=\"frac\"><span>5</span><span>6</span></span></b>. That is D."], "why": {"0": "5/2 is about where the line crosses the y-axis (2.5). Rate of change is rise over run between the dots.", "1": "This is run over rise (12 over −10). Rise goes on top: −10/12 = −5/6.", "2": "This uses where the line crosses the axes, and the sign is wrong. The line goes down, so the slope is negative."}, "right": "Rise over run. Going down → negative."},
20:{"how": ["The <b>y-intercept</b> is the value when <var>x</var> = 0.", "<var>h</var>(0) = 12.3(4.9)<sup>0</sup>. Anything to the 0 power is 1.", "12.3 × 1 = <b>12.3</b>."], "miss": "In y = a(b)^x, a is the y-intercept: 12.3. 4.9 is what you multiply by each step, not the starting value.", "right": "Exponential: the number in front is the y-intercept.", "numworks": "<b>Calculation</b>: type 12.3×4.9^0, press the right arrow, then EXE. It shows 12.3."},
21:{"how": ["Divide the numbers: 8.8 ÷ 2.2 = <b>4</b>.", "Divide the powers of 10: subtract the exponents. 9 − (−3) = 9 + 3 = <b>12</b>.", "4 × 10<sup>12</sup>. That is A."], "why": {"1": "This adds 9 + (−3) = 6. Dividing powers means subtract: 9 − (−3) = 12.", "2": "This divides 9 ÷ (−3). Subtract the exponents: 9 − (−3) = 12.", "3": "Dividing powers means subtract: 9 − (−3) = 12. Subtracting a negative is adding."}, "right": "Divide the numbers, subtract the exponents. Minus a negative is plus.", "numworks": "<b>Calculation</b>: type 8.8×10^9 ÷ (2.2×10^−3). It shows 4E12 (4 × 10¹²)."},
22:{"how": ["The question says the data is <b>quadratic</b>, so use quadratic regression with time as <var>x</var> and depth as <var>y</var>.", "The calculator gives about <var>y</var> = 0.26<var>x</var><sup>2</sup> − 3.11<var>x</var> + 0 (the last number is about 0).", "<var>d</var>(<var>x</var>) = 0.26<var>x</var><sup>2</sup> − 3.11<var>x</var>. That is C."], "why": {"0": "This is what you get if time and depth are switched in the calculator. Time goes in x.", "1": "This also comes from switching time and depth. Time is x, depth is y.", "3": "The regression’s last number is about 0, not 1. (r² = 1 is how well it fits, not part of the equation.)"}, "right": "Inputs (time) in x, outputs (depth) in y, then the regression the question names.", "desmos": "Add a table with the data, then use <b>Add Regression</b> and pick quadratic: about y = 0.26x² − 3.11x + 0.", "numworks": "<b>Regression</b>: put x in X1 and y in Y1, open the Graph tab, press OK and pick <b>Quadratic ax²+bx+c</b>. The Stats tab shows a ≈ 0.26, b ≈ −3.11, c ≈ 0."},
23:{"how": ["Add the like terms. <var>r</var><var>t</var>: 5 + 6 = <b>11</b>.", "<var>r</var><var>w</var>: −3 + (−4) = <b>−7</b>.", "<var>t</var><var>w</var>: −8 + 2 = <b>−6</b>.", "11<var>r</var><var>t</var> − 7<var>r</var><var>w</var> − 6<var>t</var><var>w</var>. That is B."], "why": {"0": "−3rw − 4rw is −7rw, not +rw. And −8tw + 2tw is −6tw.", "2": "−3rw − 4rw is −7rw, not +rw. Both are negative.", "3": "−8tw + 2tw is −6tw. The 2tw is positive."}, "right": "Combine only matching letters. Watch each sign."},
24:{"how": ["Zeros −7 and 7 give the factors (<var>x</var> + 7)(<var>x</var> − 7).", "Multiply: <var>x</var><sup>2</sup> − 7<var>x</var> + 7<var>x</var> − 49 = <var>x</var><sup>2</sup> − 49. That is A.", "Check: 7<sup>2</sup> − 49 = 0 and (−7)<sup>2</sup> − 49 = 0. ✓"], "why": {"1": "7 × (−7) = −49, not +49. And x² + 49 is never 0.", "2": "This adds −7 + −7. Multiply the last terms: 7 × (−7) = −49.", "3": "This adds 7 + 7. Multiply the last terms: 7 × (−7) = −49."}, "right": "Zero r gives factor (x − r). Multiply the factors.", "numworks": "<b>Calculation</b>: try x = 7 in each choice. Only 7² − 49 gives 0."},
25:{"how": ["The two points are <b>(−2, 6)</b> and <b>(1, −4)</b>.", "<b>Run</b>: 1 − (−2) = 3. <b>Rise</b>: −4 − 6 = −10. Slope = <b>−<span class=\"frac\"><span>10</span><span>3</span></span></b>.", "Find <var>b</var> with (1, −4): −4 = −<span class=\"frac\"><span>10</span><span>3</span></span>(1) + <var>b</var>, so <var>b</var> = −4 + <span class=\"frac\"><span>10</span><span>3</span></span> = −<span class=\"frac\"><span>12</span><span>3</span></span> + <span class=\"frac\"><span>10</span><span>3</span></span> = <b>−<span class=\"frac\"><span>2</span><span>3</span></span></b>.", "<var>y</var> = −<span class=\"frac\"><span>10</span><span>3</span></span><var>x</var> + (−<span class=\"frac\"><span>2</span><span>3</span></span>). Move −<span class=\"frac\"><span>10</span><span>3</span></span> to the first box and −<span class=\"frac\"><span>2</span><span>3</span></span> to the second."], "miss": "Slope first: from (−2, 6) to (1, −4) the line goes down 10 and right 3, so the slope is −10/3 (negative, not 10/3, and not 11/3: count the spaces between 6 and −4, not the grid lines). Then b = −2/3. The slope goes in the box in front of x.", "right": "Rise over run between the dots, then plug in a point to find b.", "desmos": "Type <b>y = −(10/3)x − 2/3</b>. It goes through (−2, 6) and (1, −4).", "numworks": "<b>Regression</b>: put x in X1 and y in Y1, open the Graph tab, press OK and pick <b>Linear ax+b</b> with the two points. The Stats tab shows a ≈ −3.33 (−10/3) and b ≈ −0.67 (−2/3)."},
26:{"how": ["The starting value is at <var>x</var> = 0: <b>9,000</b>. That is <var>a</var>.", "Each year: 8,100 ÷ 9,000 = <b>0.9</b>. Check: 7,290 ÷ 8,100 = 0.9. ✓ That is <var>b</var>.", "<var>v</var>(<var>x</var>) = 9,000(0.9)<sup><var>x</var></sup>. That is B."], "why": {"0": "9,000 is right, but 1.1 would make the value grow. The value goes down, so b is less than 1: 8,100 ÷ 9,000 = 0.9.", "2": "The start is at x = 0 (9,000), not x = 1. And 1.1 would make it grow.", "3": "0.9 is right, but the start is at x = 0: 9,000."}, "right": "a = value at x = 0. b = next ÷ previous.", "numworks": "<b>Regression</b>: put x in X1 and y in Y1, open the Graph tab, press OK and pick <b>Exponential a*b^x</b>. The Stats tab shows a = 9,000, b = 0.9."},
27:{"how": ["Two numbers that multiply to <b>−22</b> and add to <b>9</b>: <b>11</b> and <b>−2</b>.", "(<var>x</var> + 11)(<var>x</var> − 2) = 0, so <var>x</var> = −11 or <var>x</var> = 2.", "The positive solution is <b>2</b>."], "miss": "The factors are (x + 11)(x − 2), so the solutions are −11 and 2. The question asks for the positive one: 2. Check: 4 + 18 − 22 = 0.", "right": "Factor, set each factor to 0, then pick the one the question asks for.", "desmos": "Type <b>y = x^2 + 9x − 22</b>. It crosses the x-axis at −11 and 2.", "numworks": "<b>Equations</b>: type the equation (with =), then <b>Solve the equation</b>: x = −11 and x = 2."},
28:{"how": ["Monitors: $250 each, <var>x</var> monitors: 250<var>x</var>. Keyboards: $50 each, <var>y</var> keyboards: 50<var>y</var>.", "“At most $4,500” means $4,500 or less: <b>≤ 4,500</b>.", "250<var>x</var> + 50<var>y</var> ≤ 4,500. That is B."], "why": {"0": "“At most” means spending exactly $4,500 is allowed, so use ≤, not &lt;.", "2": "The prices are switched (x is monitors at $250), and “at most” needs ≤.", "3": "The prices are switched. x is the number of monitors, which cost $250."}, "right": "Each price with its own variable. At most: ≤. At least: ≥."},
29:{"how": ["The question says a <b>linear</b> function fits, so use linear regression with months as <var>x</var> and percent as <var>y</var>.", "The calculator gives about <var>y</var> = −13.5<var>x</var> + 97.8.", "That is A. It makes sense: about 98% is left at the start, and about 13.5% gets done each month."], "why": {"1": "The slope is right, but 7.3 is about where the line would hit the x-axis. b is where it starts: about 97.8.", "2": "The slope and y-intercept are switched. The data goes down, so the slope is negative.", "3": "This uses 7.3 (about where the line hits the x-axis) as the slope. The data goes down, so the slope is negative."}, "right": "Linear regression; the slope sign matches the direction of the data.", "desmos": "Add a table with the data, then use <b>Add Regression</b> (linear): m ≈ −13.46 and b ≈ 97.81.", "numworks": "<b>Regression</b>: put x in X1 and y in Y1, open the Graph tab, press OK and pick <b>Linear ax+b</b>. The Stats tab shows a ≈ −13.46, b ≈ 97.81."},
30:{"how": ["36 = 6<sup>2</sup>, so <var>x</var><sup>2</sup> − 36 is a <b>difference of squares</b>: (<var>x</var> + 6)(<var>x</var> − 6).", "Set each factor to 0: <var>x</var> = −6 and <var>x</var> = 6.", "The zeros are −6 and 6. That is C."], "why": {"0": "(x − 6)(x − 6) = x² − 12x + 36, not x² − 36. A difference of squares is (x + 6)(x − 6).", "1": "The square root of 36 is 6, not 36 ÷ 2 = 18. And (x − 18)² isn’t x² − 36.", "3": "The square root of 36 is 6, not 36 ÷ 2 = 18. Check: 18² − 36 = 288, not 0."}, "right": "a² − b² = (a + b)(a − b). Use the square root.", "desmos": "Type <b>y = x^2 − 36</b>. It crosses the x-axis at −6 and 6."},
31:{"how": ["Replace <var>x</var> with −5: <var>f</var>(−5) = 7 − 4(−5).", "Multiply first: −4 × −5 = <b>+20</b>.", "7 + 20 = <b>27</b>. That is A."], "why": {"1": "−4 × −5 is +20, not −20. Two negatives make a positive.", "2": "Multiply before subtracting. 7 − 4 first gives 3(−5), but the 4 belongs with x.", "3": "This multiplies all three numbers. It is 7 minus 4 times −5."}, "right": "Put the number in with parentheses, then multiply before adding.", "desmos": "Type <b>f(x) = 7 − 4x</b>, then <b>f(−5)</b> on a new line. It shows 27.", "numworks": "<b>Calculation</b>: type 7 − 4×(−5). It shows 27."},
32:{"how": ["Point-slope form: <var>y</var> = <var>m</var>(<var>x</var> − <var>x</var><sub>1</sub>) + <var>y</var><sub>1</sub>. Here the slope is <b>−4</b> and the point is <b>(−3, −2)</b>.", "Find the graph that goes through (−3, −2) and goes <b>down</b> 4 for every 1 to the right: (−3, −2) to (−2, −6).", "That is C."], "why": {"0": "This goes up (slope 4) and goes through (−3, 2). The slope is −4 and the point is (−3, −2).", "1": "This goes through (−3, −2), but it goes up. The slope is −4, so the line goes down.", "3": "This goes down with slope −4, but it goes through (−3, 2). (x + 3) means x = −3, and − 2 means y = −2."}, "right": "Inside the parentheses: opposite sign for x. Outside: same sign for y.", "desmos": "Type <b>y = −4(x + 3) − 2</b>. It goes through (−3, −2) and (−2, −6)."},
33:{"how": ["Multiply 10 × 6 = 60. Find two numbers that multiply to 60 and add to −19: <b>−15</b> and <b>−4</b>.", "Split and group: 10<var>x</var><sup>2</sup> − 15<var>x</var> − 4<var>x</var> + 6 = 5<var>x</var>(2<var>x</var> − 3) − 2(2<var>x</var> − 3).", "= (5<var>x</var> − 2)(2<var>x</var> − 3). 5<var>x</var> − 2 is a factor. That is D."], "why": {"0": "Check: 10x − 3 = 0 gives x = 0.3, and 10(0.09) − 19(0.3) + 6 = 1.2, not 0.", "1": "Check: 10x − 1 = 0 gives x = 0.1, and 10(0.01) − 1.9 + 6 = 4.2, not 0.", "2": "The 3 and 2 got switched when grouping. The factors are (5x − 2)(2x − 3)."}, "right": "Multiply a × c, find the pair that adds to b, split, group.", "check": "Multiply back: (5x − 2)(2x − 3) = 10x² − 15x − 4x + 6 = 10x² − 19x + 6. ✓", "desmos": "Type <b>y = 10x^2 − 19x + 6</b>. It crosses the x-axis at 0.4 and 1.5. 5x − 2 = 0 gives x = 0.4, so 5x − 2 is a factor."},
34:{"how": ["Pick two rows: (0, 460) and (18, 604).", "<b>Run</b> (seconds): 18 − 0 = 18. <b>Rise</b> (feet): 604 − 460 = 144.", "Rate = 144 ÷ 18 = <b>8</b> feet per second."], "miss": "Divide the change in distance by the change in time: 144 ÷ 18 = 8. Don’t divide a single distance by a single time (604 ÷ 18 is not the rate), and keep feet on top.", "right": "Change in y ÷ change in x, using two rows.", "numworks": "<b>Regression</b>: put x in X1 and y in Y1, open the Graph tab, press OK and pick <b>Linear ax+b</b>. The Stats tab shows a = 8, b = 460."},
35:{"how": ["The line is flat: <b>horizontal</b>.", "<b>HOY</b>: a <b>H</b>orizontal line has slope <b>0</b> and its equation is <b><var>y</var></b> = a number.", "It crosses the y-axis at −6, so <var>y</var> = −6. That is B."], "why": {"0": "y = 0 is the x-axis itself. This line is 6 below it.", "2": "x = 0 is the y-axis (a vertical line). This line is horizontal.", "3": "x = −6 is a vertical line (VUX). This line is horizontal, so it is y = −6."}, "right": "HOY: horizontal → y = number. VUX: vertical → x = number."},
36:{"how": ["In <var>g</var>(<var>x</var>) = 18(1.3)<sup><var>x</var></sup>, 18 is the starting number and <b>1.3</b> is what you multiply by each month.", "1.3 = 1 + 0.3 = 100% + <b>30%</b>. It is more than 1, so the population <b>grows</b>.", "It increases at a rate of 30% each month. That is C."], "why": {"0": "Multiplying by 1.3 is a percent increase, not adding 13 insects each month.", "1": "1.3 is more than 1, so the population grows, and it is a percent, not 13 insects.", "3": "1.3 is more than 1, so it grows. A 30% decrease would be 0.7."}, "right": "b > 1: grows by (b − 1) as a percent. b &lt; 1: decays by (1 − b)."},
37:{"how": ["Test each point in <var>y</var> &gt; −<span class=\"frac\"><span>1</span><span>6</span></span><var>x</var> − 4. If it is true, the point is in the solution set.", "(−8, 8): −<span class=\"frac\"><span>1</span><span>6</span></span>(−8) − 4 = <span class=\"frac\"><span>4</span><span>3</span></span> − 4 ≈ −2.7. Is 8 &gt; −2.7? <b>Yes.</b>", "That is A. On the graph, (−8, 8) is above the line, and &gt; means above."], "why": {"1": "(6, −5) is right on the line: −(1/6)(6) − 4 = −5. Is −5 > −5? No. > doesn’t include the line.", "2": "−(1/6)(4) − 4 ≈ −4.7. Is −6 > −4.7? No. (4, −6) is below the line.", "3": "−(1/6)(−2) − 4 ≈ −3.7. Is −7 > −3.7? No. (−2, −7) is below the line."}, "right": "Plug the point in. y > means above the line, and on the line doesn’t count."},
38:{"how": ["The line starts at <b>(0, 32)</b>, so <var>b</var> = 32.", "Use (0, 32) and (40, 104): run 40, rise 72. Slope = <span class=\"frac\"><span>72</span><span>40</span></span> = <b><span class=\"frac\"><span>9</span><span>5</span></span></b>.", "<var>y</var> = <span class=\"frac\"><span>9</span><span>5</span></span><var>x</var> + 32. That is D."], "why": {"0": "This slope is upside down (run over rise), and it leaves out the 32 where the line starts.", "1": "The slope is right, but the line starts at 32, not 0. Add 32.", "2": "The 32 is right, but the slope is upside down. Rise over run: 9/5."}, "right": "b = where it starts on the y-axis. Slope = rise over run.", "desmos": "Type <b>y = (9/5)x + 32</b>. It goes through (0, 32) and (40, 104), like the graph."},
39:{"how": ["Get <var>y</var> alone. First: 2<var>y</var> &lt; −<var>x</var> − 2, so <var>y</var> &lt; −<span class=\"frac\"><span>1</span><span>2</span></span><var>x</var> − 1. Second: <var>y</var> &lt; <var>x</var> + 3.", "Both use &lt; (no “or equal”), so both lines are <b>dashed</b>.", "&lt; means shade <b>below</b> each line. The solution is where both are shaded: the region below both lines.", "That is C."], "why": {"0": "The shading is right, but &lt; has no “or equal,” so both lines must be dashed.", "1": "The lines are dashed, but this shades above y = −(1/2)x − 1. y &lt; means below.", "3": "This shades above y = −(1/2)x − 1 and uses solid lines. y &lt; means below and dashed."}, "right": "Solve for y. &lt; or >: dashed. Less than: below. Greater than: above.", "desmos": "Desmos on the test can’t graph inequalities. Type <b>y = −(1/2)x − 1</b> and <b>y = x + 3</b> to see the lines, then pick the side yourself."},
40:{"how": ["Zeros: <var>x</var> + 1 = 0 gives <var>x</var> = <b>−1</b>, and <var>x</var> − 3 = 0 gives <var>x</var> = <b>3</b>.", "y-intercept: <var>h</var>(0) = (1)(−3) = <b>−3</b>.", "The graph that crosses the x-axis at −1 and 3 and the y-axis at −3 is A."], "why": {"1": "This crosses the x-axis at −3 and 1. (x + 1) gives x = −1 (opposite sign).", "2": "The zeros are right, but this crosses the y-axis at −1.5. h(0) = (1)(−3) = −3.", "3": "The y-intercept is −3, but the zeros are −3 and 1. (x + 1) gives x = −1 and (x − 3) gives x = 3."}, "right": "Factor (x − r) gives zero r. Then check the y-intercept.", "desmos": "Type <b>y = (x + 1)(x − 3)</b>. It crosses the x-axis at −1 and 3 and the y-axis at −3."},
41:{"how": ["Divide each term by the one before: −16 ÷ −4 = <b>4</b>. The common ratio is 4.", "Try each choice with <var>n</var> = 1, 2, 3. <var>a</var><sub><var>n</var></sub> = −(4)<sup><var>n</var></sup>: −4, −16, −64. ✓", "The minus sign is <b>outside</b>, so every term is negative. That is B."], "why": {"0": "−4n gives −4, −8, −12. That adds −4 each time. A geometric sequence multiplies.", "2": "−n² gives −1, −4, −9. The first term should be −4.", "3": "(−4)ⁿ gives −4, 16, −64: the signs flip. Every term in the sequence is negative, so the − must be outside the power."}, "right": "Geometric: find what you multiply by, then test n = 1, 2, 3.", "numworks": "<b>Calculation</b>: type −4^2 (press the right arrow after the 2), then EXE. It shows −16, the 2nd term. (−4)^2 shows 16."},
42:{"how": ["Distribute: 4<var>q</var> + 226 = 30<var>q</var> − 112.", "<b>Slide</b> the 4<var>q</var>: 226 = 26<var>q</var> − 112. Add 112: 338 = 26<var>q</var>.", "<b>Slice</b> by 26: <var>q</var> = <b>13</b>."], "miss": "The 4 multiplies both terms: 4 × 56.5 = 226. Then get the q-terms together: 26q = 338, so q = 13. Check: 4(69.5) = 278 and 30(13) − 112 = 278.", "right": "Distribute, slide, slice, then check.", "numworks": "<b>Equations</b>: type the equation (with =), then <b>Solve the equation</b>: q = 13 (type x for q)."},
43:{"how": ["Pull out the greatest common factor, 4: 4(9<var>m</var><sup>2</sup> − 25).", "9<var>m</var><sup>2</sup> − 25 is a <b>difference of squares</b>: (3<var>m</var>)<sup>2</sup> − 5<sup>2</sup> = (3<var>m</var> − 5)(3<var>m</var> + 5).", "4(3<var>m</var> − 5)(3<var>m</var> + 5). That is B."], "why": {"0": "Multiply it out: 36m² + 45m − 80m − 100 = 36m² − 35m − 100. There should be no m term.", "2": "Multiply it out: 2(18m² + 20m − 45m − 50) has an m term. There should be none.", "3": "(3m − 5)² is a perfect square, which has a middle term (−30m). A difference of squares is (3m − 5)(3m + 5)."}, "right": "GCF first, then a² − b² = (a − b)(a + b).", "check": "Multiply back: 4(9m² − 25) = 36m² − 100. ✓"},
44:{"how": ["The question says the data is <b>exponential</b>, so use exponential regression with months as <var>x</var> and revenue as <var>y</var>.", "The calculator gives about <var>r</var>(<var>x</var>) = 223.06(1.09)<sup><var>x</var></sup>.", "That is A. It makes sense: the revenue grows, so <var>b</var> is more than 1."], "why": {"1": "The a and b are switched. a (the start) is in front, b is in the parentheses.", "2": "This is what you get if months and revenue are switched in the calculator. Also 0.92 would mean it shrinks, but revenue grows.", "3": "This switches months and revenue, and switches a and b."}, "right": "Inputs in x, outputs in y. b > 1 means growth.", "desmos": "Add a table with the data, then use <b>Add Regression</b> and pick exponential. Desmos fits it a little differently and shows about y = 256(1.08)^x. The only choice that grows (b just above 1) with a reasonable start is A.", "numworks": "<b>Regression</b>: put x in X1 and y in Y1, open the Graph tab, press OK and pick <b>Exponential a*b^x</b>. The Stats tab shows a ≈ 223.06, b ≈ 1.09."},
45:{"how": ["First: 2<var>x</var> = 6 − <var>y</var>. Add <var>y</var> and subtract 2<var>x</var>: <var>y</var> = −2<var>x</var> + 6.", "Second: 5<var>x</var> − 4<var>y</var> = 28. Subtract 5<var>x</var>: −4<var>y</var> = −5<var>x</var> + 28. Divide by −4: <var>y</var> = <span class=\"frac\"><span>5</span><span>4</span></span><var>x</var> − 7.", "Graph: y-intercept 6 going down, and y-intercept −7 going up. They cross at <b>(4, −2)</b>. That is B.", "Check: 2(4) = 8 and 6 − (−2) = 8 ✓. 5(4) − 4(−2) = 28 ✓."], "why": {"0": "These lines cross at (4, 2). Check: 2(4) = 8 but 6 − 2 = 4. The y-intercepts are 6 and −7.", "2": "These lines cross at (−4, 2). The y-intercepts here are −6 and 7; they should be 6 and −7.", "3": "These lines cross at (−4, −2). The slopes have the wrong signs: the line with y-intercept 6 goes down."}, "right": "Put both in y = form, then check the crossing point in both equations.", "desmos": "Type <b>y = −2x + 6</b> and <b>y = (5/4)x − 7</b>. Click where they cross: (4, −2).", "numworks": "<b>Grapher</b>: Add an element, type −2x + 6 and 5x/4 − 7, open the Graph tab, press the <b>Toolbox</b> key and choose <b>Intersection</b>: (4, −2)."},
46:{"how": ["Two numbers that multiply to <b>−15</b> and add to <b>2</b>: <b>5</b> and <b>−3</b>.", "(<var>x</var> + 5)(<var>x</var> − 3). That is C."], "why": {"0": "15 × (−1) = −15, but 15 + (−1) = 14, not 2.", "1": "1 × (−15) = −15, but 1 + (−15) = −14, not 2.", "3": "3 × (−5) = −15, but 3 + (−5) = −2. The signs are switched."}, "right": "Multiply to the last number AND add to the middle number.", "check": "Multiply back: (x + 5)(x − 3) = x² − 3x + 5x − 15 = x² + 2x − 15. ✓"},
47:{"how": ["<b>Domain</b> is the <var>x</var>-values. “Less than −4” means the graph is to the <b>left</b> of <var>x</var> = −4.", "−4 is not included, so there is an <b>open</b> circle at <var>x</var> = −4.", "C starts with an open circle at (−4, 0) and goes left. That is C."], "why": {"0": "This ends at x = 0 (the −4 is its y-value). Its domain is x &lt; 0.", "1": "This starts at x = −4 but goes right, so its domain is x > −4.", "3": "This starts at x = 0 and goes right. Its domain is x > 0."}, "right": "Domain = x-values, read left to right. Less than: to the left."},
48:{"how": ["<b>Run</b>: 5 − (−3) = <b>8</b>. <b>Rise</b>: 8 − 1 = <b>7</b>.", "Slope = <span class=\"frac\"><span>7</span><span>8</span></span>. That is B.", "The grid is there to help: plot the points and count if you want."], "why": {"0": "This adds instead of subtracting (5 + (−3) = 2 and 8 + 1 = 9). Slope uses differences.", "2": "This adds instead of subtracting, and the sign is wrong. Subtract: 7 over 8.", "3": "5 − (−3) is 8, not −8. The line goes up, so the slope is positive."}, "right": "Rise over run. Subtracting a negative is adding.", "desmos": "Add a table with (−3, 1) and (5, 8), then use <b>Add Regression</b> (linear): m = 0.875 = 7/8."},
49:{"how": ["Let <var>x</var> = morning route miles and <var>y</var> = afternoon route miles.", "Last month: 16<var>x</var> + 12<var>y</var> = 141. This month: 10<var>x</var> + 15<var>y</var> = 123.75.", "Solve the system: <var>x</var> = <b>5.25</b> and <var>y</var> = 4.75. The morning route is 5.25 miles. That is A.", "Check: 16(5.25) + 12(4.75) = 84 + 57 = 141. ✓"], "why": {"1": "6 is 16 − 10 (a number of trips), not a distance. Set up a system.", "2": "4.75 miles is the afternoon route (y). The question asks for the morning route.", "3": "This divides all the miles by all the trips. The two routes have different lengths, so set up a system."}, "right": "Name the unknowns, write one equation per bullet, solve, then answer what was asked.", "desmos": "Type <b>y = (141 − 16x)/12</b> and <b>y = (123.75 − 10x)/15</b>. They cross at (5.25, 4.75).", "numworks": "<b>Grapher</b>: Add an element, type (141 − 16x)/12 and (123.75 − 10x)/15, open the Graph tab, press the <b>Toolbox</b> key and choose <b>Intersection</b>: (5.25, 4.75)."},
50:{"how": ["<var>p</var> opens up with its vertex at (0, 0): <var>p</var>(<var>x</var>) = <var>x</var><sup>2</sup>.", "<var>q</var> opens <b>down</b> (a minus in front) and its vertex moved <b>up 2</b> to (0, 2).", "So <var>q</var>(<var>x</var>) = −<var>x</var><sup>2</sup> + 2. That is D."], "why": {"0": "(x − 2) inside moves the graph right 2. The vertex of q is at (0, 2): up 2, not right.", "1": "(x + 2) inside moves the graph left 2. The vertex of q is at (0, 2): up 2.", "2": "− 2 outside moves the graph down. The vertex of q is at (0, 2), so it moved up: + 2."}, "right": "Minus in front: flips upside down. Outside number: up or down.", "desmos": "Type <b>y = −x^2 + 2</b>. Its top point is (0, 2), like q."},
51:{"how": ["Distribute: 80 − 10<var>y</var> = 10<var>y</var> + 5 − 5<var>y</var>.", "Combine the right side: 80 − 10<var>y</var> = 5<var>y</var> + 5.", "<b>Slide</b> the −10<var>y</var>: 80 = 15<var>y</var> + 5. Subtract 5: 75 = 15<var>y</var>.", "<b>Slice</b> by 15: <var>y</var> = <b>5</b>. That is C."], "why": {"0": "The 2 multiplies both terms: 2 × 5y = 10y, not 5y.", "1": "2 × (−5y) is −10y, not +10y. Keep the signs when you distribute.", "3": "Solve carefully: y = 5, which is choice C."}, "right": "Distribute to every term, combine, slide, slice.", "check": "Check: 2(40 − 25) = 30 and 10(5) + 5(1 − 5) = 50 − 20 = 30. ✓", "numworks": "<b>Equations</b>: type the equation (with =), then <b>Solve the equation</b>: x = 5 (type x for y)."},
52:{"how": ["The start is $200,000, so the graph must start at <b>(0, 200,000)</b>.", "6% growth: <var>b</var> = 1 + 0.06 = <b>1.06</b>. <var>y</var> = 200,000(1.06)<sup><var>x</var></sup> grows slowly at first, then faster.", "At 20 years: 200,000(1.06)<sup>20</sup> ≈ 641,000. Graph A is about there. That is A."], "why": {"1": "This starts at 100,000 and grows way too fast (1.6 = 60% each year).", "2": "This starts at 200,000, but it grows way too fast. That is 60% each year (1.6), not 6% (1.06).", "3": "This grows at 6%, but it starts at 100,000. The home starts at 200,000."}, "right": "6% = 0.06, so b = 1.06 (not 1.6). Start at a.", "numworks": "<b>Calculation</b>: type 200000×1.06^20, press the right arrow, then EXE. It shows about 641,427."},
53:{"how": ["<b>Range</b> is the <var>y</var>-values (RBT: range goes bottom to top).", "Read each dot’s <var>y</var>-value: 96, 88, 80, 72, 64, 56, 48 (8 fewer balls for each player).", "{96, 88, 80, 72, 64, 56, 48}. That is C."], "why": {"0": "These are the numbers on the y-axis scale, not the dots. Read the y-value of each dot.", "1": "These add 8 to the number of players. The y-values are the golf balls left: 96 − 8x.", "3": "These are the x-values (number of players). That is the domain."}, "right": "Range = y-values of the actual points, not the axis labels."},
54:{"how": ["A <b>zero</b> of −2 means the line crosses the <b>x-axis</b> at (−2, 0).", "A <b>y-intercept</b> of 6 means it crosses the <b>y-axis</b> at (0, 6).", "Only B goes through both points. That is B."], "why": {"0": "This crosses the y-axis at 6, but it crosses the x-axis at 3, not −2. (Its slope is −2.)", "2": "The two numbers are switched: this crosses the x-axis at 6 and the y-axis at −2.", "3": "This crosses the y-axis at −2 and the x-axis at −6."}, "right": "Zero = x-intercept. y-intercept on the y-axis.", "desmos": "Type <b>y = 3x + 6</b>. It goes through (−2, 0) and (0, 6)."}
};
ITEMS.forEach((it,i)=>{ if(EXPLAIN[i+1]) it.explain=EXPLAIN[i+1]; });
